## What I did (RULE 4 loop on the cross_visibility `cache_coherency` blocker)

**Built the proving instrument.** Added an always-on detector **P-DIRFLUSH** in `xfs/libxfs/xfs_inode_buf.c` (`xfs_inode_buf_write_verify` → `mxfs_inode_buf_write_dirlog`). It logs, on every inode-cluster buffer *write*, the `{ino, sf count, first-name, realns}` of each shortform directory inode in the buffer. This directly targets the sess59 decisive finding (disk progresses to 4 dirents, then *reverts* to 1 ~3s later — a stale cluster-buffer writeback). Confirmed firing on the cluster (saw the count timeline: `ino=29360256 count=1` on test1, `count=2` on test2).

**Ruled out a sub-hypothesis with code evidence.** The BAST handler `mxfs_dlm_bast_process` *already* stales the cluster buffer (`xfs_buf_stale` + clear `XBF_DONE`). I proved via `xfs_ialloc.c:2700-2731` that the stale covers the whole cluster (im_blkno/im_len = full cluster; co-resident inodes share an identical imap) — so "the stale misses the buffer" is **false**.

**Sharpened the mechanism.** Staling only forces a re-read on the next *read*. When the reverting node still owns a *dirty co-resident child* (its inode-log-item on the same cluster buffer), the next op is a *write* (xfsaild flushing the child via `xfs_iflush_cluster`), which stamps only the child and writes the buffer as-is — carrying the stale dir region to disk. Located the known-safe fix site (`xfs_iflush_cluster`, per sess44's note) and the FUA-read helpers to reuse.

